# `distr::HalfCosine`

The half-cosine distribution: a single half-wave of a cosine as a density on a symmetric interval.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series distr::pdf(dst::HalfCosine, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logpdf(dst::HalfCosine, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::cdf(dst::HalfCosine, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logcdf(dst::HalfCosine, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::sample(dst::HalfCosine, const std::vector<double>& params, std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

The half-cosine distribution has a density in the shape of one half-wave of a cosine, highest at the centre and falling to zero at the ends of `[t - s, t + s]`; with the defaults (`s = pi`) the density is `cos((x - t)/2)/4`. It is a smooth, symmetric, bounded model with a more peaked centre than the raised cosine.

The kind-based functions take `{}` (centre 0, half-width pi) or `{t, s}`.

The parameters, in the order of the `params` list:

| Parameter | Constraint | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `t` | any real | the centre |
| `s` | > 0 | the half-width of the support |

Support: the interval `[t - s, t + s]`.

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape. Points outside the support have density zero and distribution value 0 or 1.

`const std::vector<double>& params`
: The parameters of the family, in the order shown in the table above (a shorter list selects the defaults: `{}` gives centre 0 and half-width pi). A list of the wrong length is refused.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` return a `Series` of the shape of `x`; `sample` returns a `Series` of the requested shape. 

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused with an error that names the requirement (`s` must be positive).
- A `params` list of the wrong length is refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 evaluates the density (mass) function, the distribution function and the log-density of the HalfCosine on a small grid with `distr::pdf`, `distr::cdf` and `distr::logpdf`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-3.0, -2.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.5, 2.5}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {};

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pdf:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::HalfCosine, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : distr::cdf(dst::HalfCosine, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpdf:";
  for (double v : distr::logpdf(dst::HalfCosine, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The density is largest on this grid at x = 0, where it is 0.25; the distribution function runs from 0.00125 at x = -3 to 0.97449 at x = 2.5, and it is the running total of the density. `logpdf` is the logarithm of the density, for example log(0.25) = -1.38629 at x = 0.

### Example 2

Example 2 checks that the three descriptions of the same probability agree: the integral of the density (sum of the masses) over a range, the difference of the distribution function at its ends, and the fraction of random draws that land in it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);
  const std::vector<double> params = {};

  // The three ways to the probability that the variable lies in [-2.5, 2.5]:
  const auto grid = pluss::series::linspace(-2.5, 2.5, 3001, f64);
  const double area = distr::pdf(dst::HalfCosine, grid, params).antidiff(grid).trapezoid().item<double>();

  const auto edges = pluss::make_series({-2.5, 2.5}, f64);
  const auto c = distr::cdf(dst::HalfCosine, edges, params).host<double>();

  const auto draws = distr::sample(dst::HalfCosine, params, {200000}, f64, 7ULL);
  const double fraction = ((draws >= -2.5).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * (draws <= 2.5).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).mean().item<double>();

  std::cout << "integral of the density: " << area << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf(2.5) - cdf(-2.5): " << c[1] - c[0] << '\n';
  std::cout << "fraction of 200000 draws in range: " << fraction << '\n';
}
```

The integral of the density (0.94898) and the difference of the distribution function (0.94898) agree to all printed digits: they are two computations of one probability. The fraction of random draws (0.94856) differs from it by 4.20e-04, which is the size of the sampling error of a fraction of 200000 draws (a standard error of about 4.92e-04), so the density, the distribution function and the sampler describe the same distribution.

### Example 3

Example 3 shows how the shape changes with the parameters: the density at the same points for 3 parameter settings.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-3.0, -2.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "s pi:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::HalfCosine, x, {0.0, 3.141592653589793}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "s 2 :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::HalfCosine, x, {0.0, 2.0}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "s 5 :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::HalfCosine, x, {0.0, 5.0}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Read down the rows: `s pi` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.25); `s 2` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.3927); `s 5` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.1571). The parameters move the location, the width and the shape of the curve, which is what choosing a member of the family means.

